    <section id="sleep-rhythm" class="scroll-mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-text-primary">Sleep Timing</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-text-tertiary">How much your bedtime moves, and the research index behind it</p>
      <div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="Sleep Timing" /></div>
      <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          Verve leads with the <strong class="text-text-primary">spread of your
          bedtime in minutes</strong>: how far it moves from one night to the
          next. That is the measure the 2025 AHA scientific statement names for
          regularity, it carries a real mark at
          <strong class="text-text-primary">60 minutes</strong>, and it is in a
          unit you can act on tonight. Clock times are treated as angles on a
          24-hour circle &mdash; a plain average would place 23:50 and 00:10
          nearly a full day apart and report the steadiest sleepers as the most
          erratic.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          Behind it sits the <strong class="text-text-primary">Sleep Regularity
          Index</strong>: the probability of being in the same state, asleep or
          awake, at any two clock times 24 hours apart. It is the stronger
          research metric &mdash; in UK Biobank it out-predicted sleep duration
          for mortality &mdash; but it is a research metric, reported by no
          consumer wearable and named by no guideline for individual care, and
          two accepted calculators disagree on the same nights. So it is the
          number underneath, not the headline. Its median of
          <strong class="text-text-primary">81</strong> is a percentile of one
          cohort, not a clinical threshold.
        </p>
